From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 08:24:10 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11034/11171] Refute my own mechanism for the exp6967
 quarantine

The 07:35Z entry concluded the marker scan counts model names in an artifact's
input data and that reducers therefore inherit the live-inference floor. A
controlled pair landed the same day and refutes it: exp6976 has 1,628
model-name hits against exp6967's 1,477, a shorter duration, the same substrate
family, and it passes. If marker count drove the flag it would flag harder.

What the pair does establish is sharper. Both artifacts resolve to the same
floor, min_duration_s 0.0001 with reason deterministic_verifier, and both exceed
it by four orders of magnitude. So exp6967's DURATION_TOO_SHORT is inconsistent
with the floor the linter itself assigned, meaning the flag came from a path that
returns before the floor is applied.

That is an open audit finding occurring in production. The QA-layer report's
check_duration_vs_claim entry describes exactly this: a clean deterministic
declaration receiving a recognized floor yet returning at the marker guard before
its dedicated duration check. It was named seven days ago.

Not established: why exp6967 reaches that guard and exp6976 does not, when both
carry about fifteen hundred model-name references. Something other than marker
presence decides it and I have not found what.

Also records why my earlier negative control was not one. exp6966 is live CUDA
at 37.99s, comfortably above the live floor, so its passing says nothing about
the deterministic path. exp6976 is the real control, and I have it only because a
routine hourly check surfaced a same-family artifact with the opposite outcome --
which is the argument for reading artifacts rather than counting them.

